Answer: A static variable can be initialized inside a static block. Example:

```java
class Businessman {
static float r;
static {
r = 2.0f;
}
}
```

The static variable receives memory during class initialization, and the static block assigns the required value. The execution sequence is: Class loading ↓ Static variable gets memory ↓ Default value assigned ↓ Static block executes ↓ r becomes 2.0

Answer: A variable should be made static when its value is common to all objects of a class or when the same variable needs to be shared among the objects. Example:

```java
class Businessman {
int p;
int t;
float si;
static float r;
}
```

Here: p can differ between objects. t can differ between objects. si can differ between objects. r can be common to all objects. Therefore, r can be static. The interview-ready definition is: A variable should be made static when its value is common to all objects of a class or when the variable needs to be shared among all objects.

Answer: A static method cannot directly access instance variables because instance variables belong to an object and receive their memory when the object is created. A static method can exist without any object. Therefore, directly accessing an instance variable from a static context is not valid. For example:

```java
class Demo {
int x;
static void test() {
// System.out.println(x);  // invalid
}
}
```

x belongs to an object, while test() belongs to the class-level context. This is why a method that depends on instance variables should generally remain an instance method.

Answer: A generic method can be made static because its behavior does not depend on a particular object's state. For example:

```java
static double milesToKilometer(double miles) {
return miles * 1.6;
}
```

It can be called using the class name:

```java
ClassName.milesToKilometer(10);
```

No object-specific state is required. In contrast, a method that performs behavior specific to an object should generally be an instance method.

Answer: A static block is also called a static initializer. It is used for static initialization and can also be used when a set of statements needs to execute before the main() method. Basic structure:

```java
class Demo {
static {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println("World");
}
}
```

Output: Hello World The static block executes before main().

Answer: During class initialization, the JVM processes static elements before entering the main() method. Therefore, when a class contains:

```java
static {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
```

and:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println("World");
}
```

the output is: Hello World The reason is that the static block is executed before the main() method.
